{
 "schema": 1,
 "title": "Every f32 input of core/math's one-argument routines, against MPFR and between targets",
 "check": "For each one-argument routine of core/math at f32, every one of the 2^32 inputs was evaluated by compiler/tests/math/evaluate.ldn on Linux x86-64 and checked against MPFR's correctly rounded result, in units in the last place: `exhaustive` gives the inputs counted, those not correctly rounded, those past the routine's fixture bound in representable floats, the largest error and an input giving it.  log_gamma is checked from +0 up and from -0 to just above -2 (parts 0:768), where its stated error holds; below -2 it loses its relative accuracy near its zeros, as its doc comment says.  Then the 2^32 results were hashed in order, every NaN as one, on Linux x86-64 natively and on Linux arm64 and RV64 under QEMU's user mode: `outputs` gives each target's SHA-256, and `outputs_agree` that all three are the same bits, so the x86-64 verdict holds for the other two.  `earlier` is the first runs of 2026-11-25 and 26, over the candidates before this tree, which recorded no code digest: their verdicts and their x86-64 output digest, and whether today's outputs are the same bits.",
 "commands": [
  "python3 scripts/math_oracle.py exhaustive FUNCTION --jobs 14 --record FILE, for each routine; log_gamma with --parts 0:768",
  "python3 scripts/math_oracle.py digest FUNCTION --jobs 10",
  "python3 scripts/math_oracle.py digest FUNCTION --target linux-arm64 --runner qemu-aarch64 --jobs 10",
  "python3 scripts/math_oracle.py digest FUNCTION --target linux-rv64 --runner qemu-riscv64 --jobs 10"
 ],
 "host": "epycmicro: NixOS, Linux 6.18, AMD EPYC 7302, 32 threads, in nix develop; QEMU 11.1's user mode for arm64 and RV64",
 "date": "2026-11-27",
